\chapter{MIR blocks, block arguments, slots, and effects}
\label{ch:79}
\index{MIR blocks, block arguments, slots, and effects}
\index{MIR!block arguments}
\index{MIR!effects}

MIR has verified control-flow blocks, SSA values, block arguments, local slots and explicit effects. An incoming edge supplies the values expected by its successor. An instruction must use appropriate types, and its operands must be available along the path. A memory slot is handled differently from a pure SSA definition. The verifier checks the supported numeric or product profile before any backend consumes it.

\section{Trace a Boolean join}
For an eligible short-circuit Boolean, the canonical decision lowering can use block arguments instead of a temporary slot. Draw the two predecessors and mark which value each passes. Verify that the inactive operand has no effects when the earlier decision short-circuits. Then emit MIR and CFG for a scalar fixture and match the drawn edges to the output. A parser accepting a managed record does not establish MIR ownership support. Use the direct C11 path when the program needs a feature outside the selected MIR capability.

\section{Transfer two arguments around a loop}
The independently constructed MIR loop in the verifier tests makes an
edge's assignment rule visible. Think of a block with parameters $p$
and $q$, initially 10 and 20. A backedge supplies the vector $(q,p)$.
Read both outgoing arguments in the old block state: the pair is
$(20,10)$. Only then bind the successor parameters. On the next visit,
printing the new values in parameter order must print 20 and then 10.
If an emitter instead writes the new $p$ first and reads that updated
value when assigning $q$, it produces 20 twice. All values still have
the correct integer type, which is why an execution test complements
the edge-type verifier.

Change the type of one destination parameter to Boolean while retaining
the original incoming integer. This time the module must fail verification
before execution: there is no implicit truth conversion on a block edge.
Reordering the physical block list likewise cannot change which parameters
are bound; targets and their arguments identify control transfers independently
of the order in which a printer lists blocks.

In the product variant each parameter carries an integer and a floating
value. Put opposite signed zeros in the two products. Checking only the
integers will detect a serial swap but miss an emitter that reconstructs
both floats as positive zero. The evaluator fixture inspects the signs
as well. Parallel transfer transports the original typed values, not just
the numerical information a particular consumer happened to print first.
The fixture round-trips the product module through serialization as well;
the new module must describe that same transfer without relying on shared
addresses in the test process.

